; Berzerk.
; See docs/berzerk-design.md.
;
;   load "berzerk
;   berzerk
;
; M6 IS THE CAMPAIGN, THE SOUND AND THE
; VOICE, and with them the game acquires
; the two things a cabinet has that a
; prototype does not: it tells you how
; you are doing, and it makes a noise
; while you do it.
;
; THE MILESTONE'S GATE is six items
; (design section 19): both difficulty
; tables, lives, the bonus, the attract
; screen, the effects off the ROM, and
; section 14.2's four fixed sentences
; spoken and captioned. Lives arrived
; early, at M4, because the ramp had
; nothing else to stop it.
;
; FOUR THINGS M6 FOUND, each written out
; where it is coded:
;
; THE SOUND IS A BYTECODE AND IT
; DISASSEMBLES. Section 14.1 named the
; interpreter at $1D12 and left the
; frequencies "off the disassembly at
; M6"; its sixteen opcodes are commented
; in this tree and all five programs
; walk straight out of them -- see
; `fx`. The chip is the Exidy sound
; board MAME gives this cabinet: a 6840
; triple timer whose outputs are square
; waves at f = 447443 / (TMR + 1), a
; noise mode on channel one, and three
; bits of volume a channel.
;
; THE ONE NUMBER THAT IS NOT READ IS THE
; TICK. The interpreter runs once an
; NMI and nothing in the ROM says how
; often that is, so every effect's
; LENGTH is a count of yields and
; nothing else. At 60 Hz the robot's
; explosion is five and a half seconds
; and the extra life is thirteen, which
; are not sounds. At 480 -- eight NMIs a
; video frame -- they are 0.69 s and
; 1.67, and all five effects land where
; a person would put them. So the tick
; is 2.083 ms, pinned by the five
; durations rather than by a schematic.
; This is `b.spd`'s position one
; milestone later: one number, in one
; place, that an emulator can overturn.
;
; THE COLOUR COLUMN COSTS NOTHING, and
; section 13.1 had priced it at ~140 ms
; a band. That paragraph was written
; when a costume baked its own pixels
; and a recolour meant eight `snapsh`
; captures. Every pixel of every costume
; in this file is `fe` -- the WEARING
; TURTLE'S PEN -- so a difficulty band
; is one `setpc` before the walls and
; another before the man. See `dt.col`.
;
; AND THE VOCABULARY IS NOT A PROPERTY
; LIST. Section 14.2 wrote `speak` as a
; `gprop` per word, because it was
; written before `say` existed. `say`
; takes a LIST or a word, and its
; letter-to-sound rules get all thirteen
; words of the four sentences right --
; checked one at a time with `phonemes`,
; which is what that operation is for.
; So a sentence is a sentence, and the
; property list is thirteen globals and
; a procedure this file does not spend.
;
; M5 IS EVIL OTTO, and with him the room
; acquires a clock. Everything else in
; this game will wait for you: the crowd
; is slow because it is a crowd (B75),
; and the maze is the same maze however
; long you stand in it. Otto arrives on
; a timer, walks through walls, kills
; robots by touching them, and cannot be
; shot -- so from this milestone the
; game asks you to leave.
;
; THE MILESTONE'S GATE is three phrases
; (design section 19): the timer
; arithmetic, walls ignored, robots
; eaten. Two of them you close with your
; eyes. The timer is on the readout as
; `OT`, counting down in units of two
; thirds of a second.
;
; THREE THINGS M5 FOUND, each written
; out where it is coded:
;
; HIS SPRITE DECODES AFTER ALL, which is
; section 7.5's "gap rather than a
; choice" closed rather than deferred
; again. The pattern table at $120B
; reads as nonsense little-endian and as
; sixteen exact frames BIG-endian, and
; the sprite fetch at $2765 says which:
; it loads the high byte first. What
; comes out is six frames of arrival and
; a ten-frame hop -- and the hop's
; HEIGHT is not in the pixels. A sprite
; whose first byte has bit 7 set carries
; a video-RAM offset in front of it
; ($2772), the row stride is 32 bytes,
; and the five values in the table are
; 32, 16, 8, 4 and 2 rows down. So nine
; of his sixteen frames are one bitmap
; at five heights, and Otto is eight
; costume slots rather than sixteen. See
; `shapes.otto`.
;
; HE IS A VECTOR FROM THE MOMENT THE
; ROOM IS BUILT, WHICH TAXES THE CROWD
; BEFORE HE EVER APPEARS. $2154 links
; him into the round-robin as the last
; act of placing the robots, and the
; interrupt spends his turn whether he
; is visible or not. So B75's
; denominator is one larger than B75
; said everywhere, and the last robot in
; a room is sharing with Otto rather
; than having the room to himself. See
; `place.robots`.
;
; AND HIS TPRIME IS 2 AND NOT
; ROBOT_SPEED ($2AEB), which is the
; PLAYER'S number. Alone in a cleared
; room he moves at exactly the man's
; thirty pixels a second -- he does not
; outrun you, he corners you, and he is
; faster than a robot only in the first
; three rooms.
;
; THE COSTUME CEILING MOVED AGAIN, 23 to
; 25, and the arithmetic that set it at
; 23 is why: it counted the robot's four
; explosion frames, which take no slot
; at all, and left Otto out of the
; inventory entirely. Twenty-five is now
; the whole cabinet -- nine of the man,
; eight of the robot, eight of Otto --
; and there are no spare slots.
;
; M4 IS THE BOLTS, both kinds, and with
; them the game stops being a dodging
; exercise. Seven slots in one array --
; two the player's and five the robots'
; -- the three firing windows, the
; player's five shooting poses, and the
; first row of the score, because RBOLTS
; is read out of the difficulty table by
; SCORE and "robots do not shoot in the
; first maze" IS that lookup.
;
; THE MILESTONE'S GATE is one sentence
; (design section 19): both bolt kinds,
; the three firing windows, and the
; alignment rule confirmed. Two of the
; three you close with your eyes. The
; third is a reading of the ROM and it
; is written up beside `b.spd`.
;
; THREE THINGS M4 FOUND, each written
; out where it is coded:
;
; THE ARRAY IS PROCESSED THREE TIMES A
; TICK AND NOT TWICE, which is design
; section 10.2's one openly-inferred
; mechanism corrected. There is a third
; pass and it is a FALL-THROUGH -- `ld
; b,7` with no `call` under it -- so a
; player bolt is nine steps a frame at
; every difficulty and a robot bolt is
; three or six. Section 10.2 expected
; them to be equal at the top of the
; table and they never are.
;
; THE ROM'S EIGHT SHOOTING SPRITES ARE
; NOT FOUR MIRRORED PAIRS. Section 7.4
; says they are and the fifteen costume
; slots were balanced on it. Reversed
; byte for byte only ONE pair is exact,
; and two of the four the section names
; are not pairs at all. The count still
; comes to five, because the pairs are
; different ones -- see `shapes.shoot`,
; which is where the arithmetic is
; redone.
;
; A BOLT DOES NOT HIT WHOEVER FIRED IT,
; which the cabinet never had to say.
; It erases the player before it moves
; the bolts and it stops a firing robot
; dead; neither survives a nine-pixel
; step out of a spawn point that is on
; the firer's own box. Section 17 gets a
; row.
;
; READ ON A BOARD 2026-08-30: HUD 26,
; FRAME 56, WORST 114, ROOM 24 at `fast`,
; AND SECTION 22'S Q2 IS ANSWERED. An
; ordinary frame with eleven robots AND
; bolts in the air is 56 ms -- 17.9 fps
; -- against M3's 53 with nothing firing,
; so the whole bolt system costs about
; three milliseconds where the projection
; allowed five. And a person playing it
; called 17.9 fps very good, which is the
; half of that question no measurement
; supplies: section 15.3 chose 50 ms and
; 20 fps "by argument rather than
; measurement", and the argument has now
; been tested. THE GATE IS 55 MS AND 18
; FPS AND ELEVEN ROBOTS STAY.
;
; WORST 114 WAS NOT ANSWERED and it was
; a hitch you can see: two frame periods
; and a bit, on every doorway and every
; death, against ROOM 24 plus a 56 ms
; body. M6 took the instrument out and
; two of the things known to be in that
; number with it -- the room number
; written INSIDE the build frame, and
; the readout the build frame carried --
; so it wants reading again from a
; revert rather than reasoning about
; from here.
;
; THREE DEFECTS CAME BACK WITH IT and all
; three are fixed, each beside the code
; that had them.
;
; B72, AND IT IS THE DECISION ABOVE
; COMING BACK: a bolt killed robots
; through a wall, and diagonally past the
; point of a wall it had visibly stopped
; on. Nine pixels in one step makes
; wall-versus-actor an ORDER and M4 chose
; actors first, deliberately -- and
; neither order is the answer. Clipping
; is: find where along the step the wall
; is, shorten the swept segment to end
; there, then look for actors. See
; `bolt.crossed?`.
;
; B73, WHICH IS B69 ONE MILESTONE LATER:
; a shot fired ALONG a wall ran down the
; line it is drawn on, crossing nothing,
; and the erase took the wall away with
; it. A crossing test cannot see
; something moving parallel to the edge
; it is asking about. See `bolt.wall?`,
; which now also asks the cabinet's own
; question -- may this pixel be here --
; in two `modulo`s.
;
; B71, THE EXPLOSION'S WESTMOST PIXEL,
; which is small; its test is not. See
; `boom`.
;
; AND B75, WHICH IS THE FINDING OF THE
; MILESTONE AND IS NOT A BOLT AT ALL. A
; recording of the cabinet said the
; robots move about once a second at the
; start of a game, against a port moving
; them fifteen pixels a second -- ten
; times too fast. ROBOT_SPEED is not one
; robot's period, it is the period the
; whole CROWD shares, because the
; interrupt moves the man and exactly ONE
; other object per video frame. It is
; written out beside `rob.vecs`, and what
; falls out of it is most of the game:
; the crowd is slow because it is a
; crowd, killing robots speeds up the
; survivors, and a robot is never faster
; than the man until he is the last one
; left.
;
; AND A SECOND PLAY SESSION SAID THE GAME
; GETS VERY HARD VERY QUICKLY, which is a
; missing game over rather than a
; difficulty curve. The ramp was checked
; against the ROM before anything was
; changed and it is FAITHFUL: both the
; doorway path ($2209 ends `jp $20D7`)
; and the per-life entry ($1806) fall
; into $209D's counter block, so every
; room and every death advances the
; threshold and speeds the robots up, and
; ROBOT_SPEED reaches its floor of 1 in
; four room builds and stays there --
; which is the step rate the whole crowd
; SHARES (B75), not one robot's. What the
; cabinet has and this did not is the
; BOUND: three lives, then the game ends
; and $187F puts the threshold, the
; speed, the bolts and the score back.
; The lives are beside `score`; B74, a
; death sending you to a NEW room, is
; beside `step.death`.
;
; M3 IS THE ROBOTS. Up to eleven of them
; walk straight at you and everything
; else they do follows from that: they
; slide into walls on corners, they walk
; into each other, and they walk into
; you. Nothing in this milestone shoots
; -- the bolts are M4 and Otto is M5 --
; so a robot's whole repertoire is
; arriving.
;
; THE MILESTONE'S GATE is four sentences
; (design section 19): seek, `IQ`, the
; count cycle, and robots killing
; robots. Three of them you close with
; your eyes -- robots come at you, they
; do not walk through drawn walls, and
; the room is a different size of crowd
; every time you cross a doorway. What
; the host cannot see is in
; tests/test_berzerk.c.
;
; FOUR THINGS M3 DECIDED, each written
; out where it is coded:
;
; THE WALL PROBE RE-RUNS ON A CELL
; CROSSING and not on a frame, which is
; design section 6.3's correction and
; the saving this milestone owes the
; budget. M0 measured `iq`'s four
; `cell.at` calls a robot a frame as the
; largest single item in the logic pass,
; and a robot moves at most three steps
; inside a 48 x 68 cell. The cache key
; is TWO corners rather than four and
; that is exact rather than a heuristic,
; so six `cell.at` calls a robot become
; three -- see `iq`, which also carries
; the one shortcut that must NOT be
; tightened.
;
; A ROBOT MOVES IN WHOLE PIXELS, off the
; arcade's own reload counter ($27A9,
; ROBOT_SPEED at $20E1) rather than at
; the fractional step-per-frame the man
; uses. That is the more faithful port
; AND it is what keeps the word table
; closed: `.setitem` of a number the
; workspace has not held before interns
; a word (B52), and eleven robots
; writing fractional coordinates into
; two lists every frame is the fuse M0
; died of, lit eleven times.
;
; THE EXPLOSION COSTS NO COSTUME SLOT,
; which is design section 7.6's question
; answered by section 18's arithmetic:
; $103B's four frames would be four
; slots and the fifteen are spoken for.
; So it is the Vectrex's random strokes
; -- and they stay INSIDE the robot's
; own 8 x 12, so his own eraser reaches
; them and no wide pen spills onto a
; wall (B67).
;
; THE COUNT IS A FIVE-ROOM CYCLE and the
; robots are the one thing in a room
; that is NOT a function of where you
; are. The maze still is; the threshold
; at $434A moves on a room, so walking
; out and back gives you the same walls
; and a different crowd. A room you
; could clear by leaving it would not be
; a room.
;
; M2 IS THE MAN, and his three sentences
; still hold: walls kill, doors work,
; and the eight directions read right on
; the keyboard.
;
; THE ROOM IS STILL A PURE FUNCTION of
; ROOM.X and ROOM.Y (design section
; 6.1), which is M1's gate and still
; the best thing in the Berzerk ROM: an
; infinite maze costs two globals and no
; storage, because the maze is thrown
; away at the doorway and generated
; again from the room's own coordinates.
;
; THREE THINGS M2 DECIDED, each written
; out where it is coded:
;
; THE COSTUME BUDGET, which is design
; section 18's third ceiling and the one
; the bitmap decision introduced.
; `COSTUME_SLOTS` is 15 and the cabinet
; has far more sprites than that: the
; man alone is sixteen. `setrot "flip`
; is the answer -- the ROM's own
; left-facing frames are hand-mirrored
; copies of its right-facing ones, for
; the robot as much as for the man --
; and the whole inventory then fits in
; fifteen with two to spare. The table
; is beside `shapes.man`.
;
; THE CONTACT BOX IS THE SPRITE, grown
; one step on every side, where design
; section 8.3 asked for the Vectrex's
; plus-or-minus four. A pixel intercept
; on a man who is 8 wide and 16 TALL is
; not a square, and the extra step is
; also what keeps the eraser off the
; walls.
;
; AND THE BORDER IS A POSITION TEST
; rather than a table lookup, because
; `cell.at` clamps and because the mask
; table has no doorways in it -- it is
; what robots consult, and robots never
; leave the room.
;
; THE KEYBOARD IS READ AS STATE from
; this milestone on. M1 read a character
; stream and was right to: one press was
; one room, and key state read in a loop
; with nothing else to do reports the
; same tap three or four times, which
; would have walked you three rooms from
; one press. A man who walks while an
; arrow is HELD cannot be driven that
; way, so `pollkeys` and `keydown?`
; arrive here with the frame loop that
; needs them.
;
; THE FRAME IS PACED BY `sync` at 20 fps
; (design section 15.3's gate), and it
; erases in place: rub the man out where
; he is, move him, put him back. Design
; section 3 settled that once the
; figures became stamps, and with one
; moving figure and a static maze the
; alternative would redraw sixteen wall
; runs to erase eight steps of man.
;
; THE INSTRUMENT IS GONE AT M6 and its
; readings are in the roadmap, which is
; where they always had to be copied by
; hand anyway. M2 to M5 carried a
; readout under the picture -- the
; fifteen wall masks, and HUD / FRAME /
; WORST / ROOM / SL / OT / ROB once a
; second -- and every board reading this
; game has was read off it: an ordinary
; frame at eleven robots is 53 ms at M3,
; 56 at M4 with bolts in the air, 50 at
; M5, against a gate restated at 55
; (design section 22, Q2). Rows 25 to 28
; are the player's now, and what is left
; on them is the cabinet's own: the
; score, the bonus, the lives and what
; the robots are saying.
;
; It cost four procedures, thirteen
; globals and a `ticks` call in each of
; `play.frame`, `draw.room` and
; `slide.room`. Putting it back is a
; revert and not a rebuild, and the next
; milestone that needs a number should
; do exactly that rather than invent a
; second instrument.

;=====================================
; The playfield, at 1:1 with the cabinet
; (design section 5)
;
;   turtle x = arcade x - 126
;   turtle y = 142 - arcade y
;
; 244 arcade pixels of 320 across and
; 204 of the 240 rows `splitscreen`
; presents, centred at (0, +40). Every
; constant in the disassembly then
; transfers verbatim in x and NEGATED in
; y, and that sign is the one thing here
; worth checking twice.
;
; The 5 x 3 cell grid: column boundaries
; at turtle x = -70, -22, 26, 74 and row
; boundaries at turtle y = 74, 6.

; THREE COLOURS AND THE WALLS ARE NOT
; ONE OF THE VARIABLE ONES, which is
; $3702 read a bit at a time rather than
; a sentence at a time.
;
; C.WALLS' compositing is
;
;   attribute = (~screen & c) | (screen & $44)
;
; over a byte that is TWO 4 x 4
; attribute boxes, one to a nibble
; ("Color look-up (RGBI,RGBI)"). $44 is
; bit 2 of each nibble and bit 2 is
; BLUE, so a box holding a wall pixel is
; forced to $4 -- blue, and only blue,
; because `~screen & c` is zero for
; exactly the bits the wall set. A box
; with NO wall pixel keeps the
; difficulty colour `c`.
;
; SO THE WALLS ARE BLUE IN EVERY ROOM
; AT EVERY SCORE, and what the fifth
; column actually colours is everything
; drawn in an empty box: the robots,
; Otto and both kinds of bolt. The port
; had this the other way round for a day
; and a board caught it.
;
; The wall sprite is 4 x 4 ($269B) and
; every wall coordinate is a multiple of
; four, so a wall lands on the attribute
; grid exactly and the trick is not
; approximate the way a bitwise trick
; over a bitmap usually is.
;
; A doorway is a plain gap -- $25D4
; draws 96 pixels of border, skips 48
; and draws 96 more -- so the boxes in
; it hold no wall pixel and carry the
; difficulty colour like any other empty
; ground. That is not a mark on the door
; and nothing in the ROM closes one: you
; can always walk back, and section 6.1
; says the room you walk back into is
; the same maze.
; 98 is Tailwind's blue-700, which is
; the nearest this palette comes to a
; non-intensified RGBI blue -- clearly
; blue, clearly not bright. The exact
; shade is a board call and it is one
; number.
make "wall.pc 98    ; $44, blue, always
make "rob.pc  254   ; the difficulty band
make "man.pc  249   ; $AA, bright green

; AND HE CHANGES COLOUR WHILE HE DIES,
; which is $27EB and is two
; instructions: `rlca` then `xor $11` on
; PLAYER_COLOUR every step of the death
; animation. From $AA that is an
; eight-colour cycle that closes exactly
; -- $44, $99, $22, $55, $BB, $66, $DD
; and back to $AA -- so the
; electrocution is not a flicker in the
; cabinet, it is the man running through
; the whole palette. Held here in the
; order the eight DRAWS want it, because
; `p.dying` counts down: `1 + int
; (p.dying / 2)` is 8 on the first drawn
; frame and 1 on the last.
make "fry.pc (list 249 253 84 251 131 60 248 98)

; WINDOW, not the default `wrap`. It
; costs nothing to set now and M2 cannot
; do without it: the man's contact box
; hangs four steps past him, and at the
; right-hand wall a wrapped probe lands
; on the far side of the screen.
window

;=====================================
; The room (design section 6)

make "room.x 0
make "room.y 0
make "seed   0

; MAZE_ZONES, 15 bytes, one per cell,
; five wide and row-major:
;
;   bit 0 (1) = wall on this cell's LEFT
;   bit 1 (2) = wall on its RIGHT
;   bit 2 (4) = wall on its TOP
;   bit 3 (8) = wall on its BOTTOM
;
; Built with `list` and never written as
; a literal in a procedure body: a
; literal belongs to the stored body of
; whatever holds it, and `.setitem`
; would scribble on it for good.
make "cell []

; The eight interior segments, one per
; interior grid intersection: start
; point in turtle steps, heading and
; length. Four lists of eight rather
; than thirty-two names, which is design
; section 18's arithmetic on the
; 254-global ceiling.
make "seg.x (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)
make "seg.y (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)
make "seg.h (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)
make "seg.l (list 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0)

; The generator at $2678: a 16-bit LCG
; whose output is the high byte. It has
; to be THIS one and not `random`,
; because the whole point is reproducing
; the room, and `rerandom` reseeds a
; different stream that is not
; indexable by room coordinate.
;
; 7 * 65535 + 12627 is 471,372, well
; inside the 2^24 a single-precision
; float holds exactly, so the modulo is
; not rounding anything away.
to rnd
  make "seed modulo ((7 * :seed) + 12627) 65536
  output int (:seed / 256)
end

to wall.set :idx :bit
  .setitem :idx :cell bitor (item :idx :cell) :bit
end

; The template at $268C, `ldir`-copied
; into MAZE_ZONES at $2567 before the
; eight segments set their bits into it.
;
; THE BORDER CELLS ARE WALLED ON ALL
; FOUR OUTER SIDES EVEN WHERE THE EXITS
; ARE -- cell (row 1, col 0) carries a
; LEFT wall although the left doorway is
; exactly there. That is not a bug. The
; table is what ROBOTS consult, and
; robots never leave the room; the
; player's exits are his own position
; test (design section 8.3, M2), which
; is why the arcade needs both.
to wall.template
  output (list 5 4 4 4 6 1 0 0 0 2 9 8 8 8 10)
end

; One intersection. `c` is the column
; boundary 1-4 (between cells c-1 and c)
; and `r` the row boundary 1-2; `k` is
; which of the eight slots this segment
; goes in.
;
; The four choices are the `set`
; instructions at $2617-$264B against
; ix+0, ix+1, ix+5 and ix+6 -- an index
; into the 5-wide array where +1 is the
; next column and +5 the next row, which
; is what makes the mapping legible:
;
;   rand & 3 = 2  horizontal, one cell
;     RIGHT: bottom of above-right, top
;     of below-right
;   rand & 3 = 3  horizontal, one cell
;     LEFT: bottom of above-left, top of
;     below-left
;   rand & 3 = 0  vertical, one cell
;     ABOVE: right of above-left, left
;     of above-right
;   rand & 3 = 1  vertical, one cell
;     BELOW: right of below-left, left
;     of below-right
;
; That is the entire maze generator.
; Nothing checks connectivity and
; nothing prevents a sealed pocket, and
; the rooms it makes are the rooms
; everybody remembers.
;
; FIFTY-TWO, NOT FORTY-EIGHT, FOR A RUN
; WEST OUT OF THE FIRST INTERSECTION,
; and it is the one number in this
; procedure that is not the ROM's
; (B70). The leftmost column is 52 steps
; wide and the other four are 48 -- the
; playfield is 244 across as
; 52 + 4 x 48, which `draw.border`
; already knows, since its top run is
; 100 + 48 + 96 rather than a symmetric
; 98 + 48 + 98. So a run of 48 westward
; from x = -70 stops four steps short of
; the left wall.
;
; THE CABINET DOES NOT HAVE THE GAP
; BECAUSE ITS WALL IS FOUR PIXELS THICK.
; $2620 steps x back 48 and lays twelve
; 4-pixel sprites, covering arcade x 8
; to 55, and the left border wall
; occupies 4 to 7: contiguous. Our
; border is a one-pixel pen line, so the
; span a run has to reach is 52. Nothing
; on the right-hand side needs it -- a
; run east out of the last intersection
; lands exactly on the right wall -- and
; nothing vertical does either, because
; all three rows are 68.
;
; The MASK is unaffected and was always
; right: `wall.set` names the cells
; either side of the run, not its
; length. That is why the picture and
; the table agreed while the picture had
; a hole in it.
to room.seg :k :c :r
  local "w local "ix local "iy
  local "a local "b
  make "w bitand rnd 3
  make "ix (48 * :c) - 118
  make "iy 142 - (68 * :r)
  make "a (5 * (:r - 1)) + :c
  make "b (5 * :r) + :c
  .setitem :k :seg.x :ix
  .setitem :k :seg.y :iy
  if 2 = :w [
    .setitem :k :seg.h 90
    .setitem :k :seg.l 48
    wall.set (:a + 1) 8
    wall.set (:b + 1) 4
    stop]
  if 3 = :w [
    .setitem :k :seg.h 270
    ifelse 1 = :c [.setitem :k :seg.l 52] [.setitem :k :seg.l 48]
    wall.set :a 8
    wall.set :b 4
    stop]
  if 0 = :w [
    .setitem :k :seg.h 0
    .setitem :k :seg.l 68
    wall.set :a 2
    wall.set (:a + 1) 1
    stop]
  .setitem :k :seg.h 180
  .setitem :k :seg.l 68
  wall.set :b 2
  wall.set (:b + 1) 1
end

; RNG_SEED := ROOM_X + 256.ROOM_Y at the
; top of every room build ($2540), so
; the maze -- and, from M3, the robot
; placement that follows it -- is a pure
; function of where you are.
;
; CREATE_ROOM ($25EB) is called twice,
; once per interior row boundary, and
; each call steps x by 48 while x < 220,
; so x = 56, 104, 152, 200. Eight
; intersections in all, and THE ORDER
; MATTERS: it is the order the generator
; is drawn from, and the room only
; reproduces if it is kept.
to setup.room
  make "seed :room.x + (256 * :room.y)
  make "cell wall.template
  room.seg 1 1 1
  room.seg 2 2 1
  room.seg 3 3 1
  room.seg 4 4 1
  room.seg 5 1 2
  room.seg 6 2 2
  room.seg 7 3 2
  room.seg 8 4 2
end

; WALLINDEX ($1CE7): a point to a cell
; index, 1-based. This is the room's
; whole public interface from M2 on --
; the cabinet did wall collision by
; DRAWING, with sprites XOR-ed into
; magic RAM and a hardware intercept
; bit, and we have no such bit and must
; not fake one with `colourunder`.
;
; The clamps are not defensive padding.
; M3's `iq` probes points four steps
; outside a robot, which at a wall are
; outside the playfield, and the arcade
; clamps there too.
to cell.at :x :y
  local "c local "r
  make "c int ((:x + 118) / 48)
  make "r int ((142 - :y) / 68)
  if 0 > :c [make "c 0]
  if :c > 4 [make "c 4]
  if 0 > :r [make "r 0]
  if :r > 2 [make "r 2]
  output (5 * :r) + :c + 1
end

;=====================================
; The walls (design section 7.1)
;
; Sixteen runs at most: eight of border
; and up to eight interior. The border
; is one closed circuit with the pen
; toggling -- twelve statements and no
; `setpos` after the first, against
; sixteen `setpos`-and-`fd` pairs if
; each run were placed on its own.
;
; The doorway is the middle cell of each
; side: 48 steps wide top and bottom, 68
; tall left and right. The top run is
; 100 + 48 + 96 rather than a symmetric
; 98 + 48 + 98 because the cabinet's
; leftmost column is 52 arcade pixels
; and the other four are 48.
;
; PEN SIZE STAYS 1. A 2-pixel pen reads
; better and costs round caps that spill
; outside the stroke, which is the
; artefact that once made a present-cost
; harness measure every frame as a full
; screen (docs/hardware-notes.md 9.1).
;
; AND THE PICTURE MOVES, which is all
; the scroll wants from this pair.
; `ox` and `oy` displace the whole room
; -- border and segments together -- and
; a room build passes 0 0. The corner is
; `setx`/`sety` rather than the literal
; `setpos [-122 142]` for the same
; reason a frame conses nothing: a
; displaced corner would be a fresh
; two-element list forty times a
; doorway.
to draw.border :ox :oy
  pu setx (:ox - 122)  sety (:oy + 142)  seth 90 pd
  fd 100  pu fd 48  pd fd 96
  rt 90
  fd 68  pu fd 68  pd fd 68
  rt 90
  fd 96  pu fd 48  pd fd 100
  rt 90
  fd 68  pu fd 68  pd fd 68
  pu
end

to draw.walls :ox :oy
  local "i
  draw.border :ox :oy
  repeat 8 [
    make "i repcount
    pu setx (item :i :seg.x) + :ox
    sety (item :i :seg.y) + :oy
    seth item :i :seg.h
    pd fd item :i :seg.l
  ]
  pu
end


;=====================================
; The man is the cabinet's own sprite
; (design section 7.4)
;
; The bytes below are the disassembly's,
; verbatim, with their addresses. Each
; is one byte wide (8 pixels) and $10
; rows tall, and the ROM reaches them
; through PLAYER_ANIMATION_TABLE
; ($2053), which has only THREE entries
; for a man who walks eight ways:
; standing ($1046), the group that runs
; up/up-right/right/down-right/down
; ($104B), and the group that runs
; down-left/left/up-left ($1056).
;
; SO THE MAN NEEDS TWO SIDES AND WE PAY
; FOR ONE, and that is this milestone's
; answer to design section 7.4. The
; ROM's own left-hand frames at $1391,
; $13A3 and $13B5 are hand-mirrored
; copies of $1089, $109B and $10AD --
; compare them byte for byte and only a
; row or two differs -- so `setrot
; "flip` produces them from the right-
; hand three. That is not a saving of
; taste: `COSTUME_SLOTS` is 15 (design
; section 18's third ceiling, the one
; the bitmap decision introduced) and
; the cabinet's inventory does not fit
; in fifteen without it.
;
; THE TWENTY-FIVE, ALLOCATED, so no
; later milestone discovers the ceiling
; the way Battlezone discovered the
; procedure one. It was fifteen until
; B77: the robots needed four more
; frames than the port had given them
; and there were two spare, so the
; ceiling moved to 23. It moved again at
; M5, and for the reason the first move
; should have made obvious -- the
; arithmetic that set it had counted the
; robot's four explosion frames, which
; take no slot at all, and had left OTTO
; out of the inventory it was adding up.
; He is eight. A slot is six bytes of
; table and the pixels come from a pool
; that is 8 KB and holds all twenty-five
; in 2,432:
;
;   1     the man, standing     $10BF
;   2-4   the man, walking      $10AD
;                               $109B
;                               $1089
;   5-9   the man, shooting     M4
;   10-13 the robot, standing,  M3
;         up, down and right
;   14-17 the robot, the rest   B77
;         of both walk cycles
;         $1147 $117C $118A
;         $111F
;   18-23 Otto, arriving        M5
;         $122E $1234 $123B
;         $1243 $124C $1256
;   24    Otto, squashed $12A7  M5
;   25    Otto, the face $129D  M5
;
; Eight shooting directions become five
; slots and five robot facings become
; four, both by the same flip: the ROM's
; robot walking left ($1155, eye row
; $1E) is exactly its robot walking
; right ($112C, eye row $78) mirrored,
; and so are their feet ($6C against
; $36). Section 7.6's explosion takes no
; slot -- the Vectrex's random dots are
; already the fallback it names -- and
; Otto's sixteen pattern-table entries
; are eight slots, because nine of them
; are the same face at five heights and
; the height is an address offset rather
; than a bitmap (see `shapes.otto`).
;
; M2 BUILT FOUR OF THE FIFTEEN and M3
; builds four more (10-13, beside
; `shapes.robots`). The five shooting
; poses arrive with the trigger in M4,
; because building them now would cost
; 5 x 18 ms of startup (section 18's
; measured figure) for a pose nothing
; can reach: there is still no bolt.
; What M2 owed section 7.4 was the
; arithmetic, and the table above is it.

; THE SPRITES ARE THE ROM'S BITMAPS,
; WRITTEN STRAIGHT IN. Two hex digits a
; pixel, `fe` the wearing turtle's pen
; colour and `ff` transparent -- which
; is byte for byte what the pen-and-
; `snapsh` path stored, because this
; game draws in colour 254 and 254 IS
; the shape pool's PEN value. The
; costumes do not change; only the way
; they are built does.
;
; WHAT IT RETIRES is `render.sprite`,
; `bit.mask` and the capture-offset
; arithmetic at both sizes: ~400
; statements and section 18's measured
; ~18 ms a sprite at startup, against a
; list the loader parses once.
;
; THE COMMENT THAT STOOD HERE SAID
; `putsh` COULD NOT DRAW AN 8-WIDE MAN,
; because it doubled every pixel
; horizontally and would take the
; playfield off section 5's 1:1. That
; was the old monochrome `putsh`. The
; one the colour-shape model brought
; takes two hex digits a pixel and is
; 1:1, so the objection is gone.
;
; ONE SHAPE, ANY COLOUR: `fe` defers to
; the stamping turtle's pen, so the
; cabinet's per-room robot colours cost
; no slot when M5 wants them.
;
; THREE PROCEDURES AND NOT ONE. `load`
; accumulates a `to ... end` into a
; 4 KB buffer before defining it
; (B69's family, found on main the same
; week), and the man's nine shapes are
; 2.9 KB of text on their own.
make "man.cyc  (list 2 3 4 3)

to shapes.man
